WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Before you seat people or split them into teams, there is a smaller question:

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.400
who is closest to whom?

3
00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:16.972
Count shared group chats, and your closest person is… the boss.

4
00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:24.210
A chip has the same problem: thousands of cells. Which two belong side by side?

5
00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:30.110
So first, give every pair a score: the higher it is, the closer they belong.

6
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:43.400
The first way: a pair's score is the weight of the wire between them.

7
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:50.000
A–B and D–E both score 5: ties go by name, whatever the input order.

8
00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:56.647
B and D share no wire: by the first rule, nothing in common.

9
00:00:56.647 --> 00:01:02.713
Both are wired to C, and a link through C is only as strong as its weaker wire:

10
00:01:02.713 --> 00:01:05.958
min(4, 2) = 2.

11
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:11.102
Check every pair: all 10 score, B–D included.

12
00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.500
Take a guess: which pair is closest?

13
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.744
X–Y has the thickest wire, 5; each triangle edge is only 4.

14
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.071
By direct wires, the first pair is X–Y;

15
00:01:24.071 --> 00:01:28.871
by shared neighbours, P–Q = 4 + 4 = 8 moves ahead.

16
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:35.578
Both answers are right: one asks how thick a wire is, the other how tight a group is.

17
00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:40.190
Remember the boss?

18
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:49.744
One five-person chat ties ten pairs at once, so it says little about any one of them.

19
00:01:49.744 --> 00:01:57.011
So share each chat out: in a five-person chat each pair gets 1/4; a private chat gives a whole 1.

20
00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:00.607
Counted this way, your closest is Lin.

21
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:08.267
Clock and reset nets reach thousands of cells: unshared, every pair looks equally close.

22
00:02:08.267 --> 00:02:13.956
In the original tiny netlist: A–B gets 0.5, E–F gets 1.

23
00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:20.560
A score only decides who merges first; the merging itself starts in the next module.

24
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:27.500
The same pair of cells gets a different score under a different rule.

25
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:35.500
Affinity is not a distance and not a probability: it is a ranking under one model.

26
00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:42.500
Next time someone says “we are the closest”, you can ask:

27
00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:48.500
by which score?

28
00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:56.600
In the lab below, change one wire's weight and watch the ranking move.
